Here is a simple sequencer patch in Sonilo.
It begins by setting up something called a "context",
which is the thing used to manage memory and create
a patch.

A context is allocated a particular address in sonilo
virtual memory. The address is stored in cursor 0.

~~~
son:ctx-create #0 son:cur son:go
~~~

Create a trigger signal to drive the sequencer.
A 125 BPM pulse is subdivided into 4 beats and fed into
a clock signal generator that produces a ramp signal. To
turn that ramp into a trigger, the metro object is used.

~~~
#125 #4 n:mul const clock metro
~~~

To build a sequence, create an array of 8 8-bit (expressed
as $k=3$, where $2^k = 2^3 = 8$) using the {\tt arr}
word, which is fed into {\tt seq}.

The prefix {\tt \%} is used to add values to the array
in ascending order.

~~~
#3 #8 arr %0 %5 %7 %10 %12 %10 %7 %5 seq
~~~

Bias by 60 (middle C). Add portamento via a smoothing
filter {\tt smoother}, and convert to MIDI via {\tt mtof}.

~~~
#60 const add #0.001 constf smoother mtof
~~~

Feed the frequency signal into a subtractive saw
oscillator ({\tt saw} and {\tt lpf}), and change
the gain. Write this to tape track 0 via {\tt tout}.

~~~
saw #200 const lpf #0.500 constf mul sink #0 tout
~~~

Open the tape track 0 ({\tt topen}), render 11 seconds
of audio ({\tt render}), and close the track ({\tt tclose}).

~~~
#0 topen #11 render #0 tclose
~~~
Destroy the context, and quit Konilo.
~~~
son:ctx-destroy bye
~~~
